# MIL-STD-1750A Architecture Support for TCC

This branch of Tiny C Compiler adds support for the `mil-std-1750a` instruction set architecture.
1750A is a 16-bit word-addressable big-endian architecture used primarily in aerospace and military systems.

## Building the Cross Compiler

To compile the cross-compiler for the 1750A architecture, you must enable cross-compilation during the configure phase and explicitly make the `1750a-tcc` target:

```sh
./configure --enable-cross
make 1750a-tcc
```

This will build a binary named `1750a-tcc` in the root of the source directory.

## Compiling Programs

Because the 1750A is treated as a bare-metal embedded system, `tcc` does not attempt to link standard C runtimes (like `crt1.o` or `libc`). Therefore, your program must provide its own entry point, `_start`.

### Example

Create a file `test.c`:

```c
int main() {
    return 42;
}

/* The linker expects a _start symbol to exist as the entry point */
int _start() {
    return main();
}
```

Invoke the cross compiler to produce a 1750A COFF file:

```sh
./1750a-tcc test.c -o test.coff
```

If you wish to only emit object files, use the `-c` flag:

```sh
./1750a-tcc -c test.c -o test.o
```

## Architecture specifics

- `char` is 16-bit (1 word)
- `short` is 16-bit (1 word)
- `int` is 16-bit (1 word)
- `long` is 32-bit (2 words)
- `float` is 32-bit (2 words)
- `double` is 48-bit (3 words)
- `long double` is 48-bit (3 words)
- `long long` (64-bit) is disabled and aliased to 32-bits (2 words).
- Pointers are 16-bit (1 word)

Inline assembly for standard instructions (`li`, `lr`, `l`, `st`, `a`, `s`, `m`, `d`, `b`, `sjs`, `urs`) is supported. Floating point instructions like `fa`, `fs`, `fm`, `fd` are also usable in inline assembly syntax.
